<docs-article
  title="CLI"
  description="marko-ui is distributed as a shadcn-compatible registry. You install components with the official shadcn CLI, pointed at our registry URLs."
  href="/docs/cli"
  toc=TOC
>
  <h2 id="no-cli">There Is No marko-ui CLI</h2>

  <p>
    This is the one place where marko-ui deliberately does less than shadcn/ui. Rather than ship
    and maintain our own command-line tool, we publish the registry in the
    <a href="https://ui.shadcn.com/schema/registry.json" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">shadcn registry format</a>
    and let the official <code>shadcn</code> CLI do the work.
  </p>

  <p>The trick that makes this work for a non-React framework:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Every file ships as <code>"type": "registry:file"</code> with an explicit
      <code>target</code> path, rather than as <code>registry:ui</code>. That is the non-React path
      through the CLI, so it copies our <code>.marko</code> files verbatim instead of running
      React-specific codemods over them.
    </li>
    <li>
      Every <code>registryDependencies</code> entry is an absolute URL into our registry. A bare
      name like <code>"utils"</code> would resolve against shadcn's own React registry and give you
      a <code>.tsx</code> file.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The consequence is that any tool speaking the shadcn registry protocol works with marko-ui —
    the CLI, editors, and agents that already know the format.
  </p>

  <h2 id="init">init</h2>

  <p>
    <code>init</code> sets up a project: it writes <code>components.json</code>, installs the
    <code>cn()</code> helper, and merges the theme's CSS variables into your stylesheet. Point it
    at a theme item so it picks up our tokens rather than shadcn's defaults.
  </p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=INIT/>

  <p>
    Swap <code>style.json</code> for <code>style-zinc.json</code>, <code>style-slate.json</code>,
    <code>style-stone.json</code>, or <code>style-gray.json</code> to start from a different base
    color. See <a href="/docs/theming">Theming</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Review the generated <code>components.json</code> afterwards — a few of its fields describe a
    React project and need adjusting for Marko. That is covered in
    <a href="/docs/components-json">components.json</a>.
  </p>

