  <p>
    <code>--dry-run</code> is worth reaching for the first time you install into an existing
    project — it lists every path that would be touched without touching any of them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="view-diff">view and --diff</h2>

  <p>
    Because you own the installed files, upgrades are a merge rather than a version bump.
    <code>view</code> is a top-level command that prints an item without writing anything;
    <code>--diff</code> is an option on <code>add</code> that compares the registry version against
    what is already on disk. Together they make that merge deliberate.
  </p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=VIEW/>

  <p>
    Once you have read the diff and decided you want it, re-add with <code>--overwrite</code>:
  </p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=OVERWRITE/>

  <p>
    <strong>This discards your local edits to that component.</strong> Commit first, overwrite,
    then re-apply your changes from the diff — the same routine as any vendored dependency.
  </p>

  <h2 id="urls">Registry URLs</h2>

  <p>Every item is a static JSON file under the same base URL:</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>URL</th>
        <th>What it is</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>/r/registry.json</code></td>
        <td>The index — every item's name, type, and file list, without file contents.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>/r/&lt;component&gt;.json</code></td>
        <td>A single component, contents included. For example <code>/r/dialog.json</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>/r/utils.json</code></td>
        <td>The <code>cn()</code> helper. Nearly every component depends on it.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>/r/style.json</code></td>
        <td>The theme: <code>globals.css</code> plus <code>cssVars</code> for the CLI to merge.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>/r/style-&lt;color&gt;.json</code></td>
        <td>The same theme in <code>zinc</code>, <code>slate</code>, <code>stone</code>, or <code>gray</code>.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>To list everything available:</p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=REGISTRY_INDEX/>

  <h2 id="item-format">Registry Item Format</h2>

  <p>A component item looks like this:</p>

  <code-block language="json" filename="/r/switch.json" code=ITEM/>

  <p>
    Note <code>marko-ui</code> in <code>dependencies</code>: the build script scans each file for
    an import of it and adds the package automatically, so a component's declared dependencies
    cannot drift out of sync with what its source actually imports.
  </p>

  <p>
    Theme items carry one extra field, <code>cssVars</code>, holding the parsed
    <code>:root</code>, <code>.dark</code>, and <code>@theme inline</code> declarations. That is
    what lets <code>init</code> merge our tokens into a stylesheet you already have instead of
    overwriting it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="build">Building the Registry</h2>

  <p>
    If you are contributing to marko-ui, or forking it to host your own registry, the JSON is
    generated from <code>packages/registry/default/</code> by one script:
  </p>

  <code-block language="bash" code=BUILD/>

  <p>
    It emits one item per directory under <code>default/ui/</code>, plus <code>utils</code>, the
    five theme items, and the <code>registry.json</code> index, into
    <code>apps/docs/public/r/</code>. <code>REGISTRY_BASE_URL</code> controls the absolute URLs
    written into <code>registryDependencies</code>; it defaults to
    <code>http://localhost:3000/r</code> so you can smoke-test the real CLI against a local dev
    server.
  </p>

  <p>
    Component metadata — title, description, and extra dependencies — comes from a
    <code>registry.meta.json</code> file next to the component source. Component directories must
    be flat; the build fails loudly on a nested directory rather than silently dropping files.
  </p>
